inal/honor focus, but different suits.\n- Consider patterns:\n  - All Honors? No \u2014 mostly suited.\n  - Full Flush? No \u2014 multiple suits.\n  - Upper Tiles (7,8,9)? Possible if I can build more high-numbered melds in Characters.\n    - I have W8 pair, W9 singleton, and melds in W4-W6 range.\n    - But W4, W5, W6 are middle tiles \u2014 not upper.\n  - So not pure upper.\n- Could go for Outside Hand (melds contain terminals or honors).\n- Or All Simples? No \u2014 I have W8, W9, T9, B9 \u2014 terminals.\n\nBut I have many terminals and honors \u2014 possibly aiming for Outside Hand or All Terminals and Honors.\n\nCheck: All Terminals and Honors (32 points): only terminals (1,9) and honors.\n\nMy hand:\n- W8: not terminal (8 is not 1 or 9) \u2192 invalid\n- So cannot achieve All Terminals and Honors.\n\nSimilarly, Full Flush impossible due to multiple suits.\n\nBest shot: perhaps develop Character suit toward higher-value outside hand.\n\nBut I have two melds already in Characters: [W4,W5,W6] and [W5,W5,W5] \u2014 wait, again, conflict.\n\nHold on: **[W5, W5, W5]** is a pung \u2014 uses three W5s.\n**[W4, W5, W6]** is a chow \u2014 uses one W4, one W5, one W6.\n\nTotal W5s: four \u2014 all used. So one W5 is shared? No, each tile is distinct. So it is possible only if the chow uses a different W5 than the pung \u2014 but they are identical tiles. In reality, you cannot have the same physical tile in two melds. But since there are four copies, you can have:\n- One copy in chow, three in pung \u2014 total four.\n\nYes, allowed. So the game state is valid.\n\nNow, what patterns can I pursue?\n\n- I have a pung of W5 (middle tile), a chow spanning W4-W6 (middle), and hand has W8, W9 (high), so not cohesive.\n\nAlternatively, maybe abandon trying to maximize score and focus on safety and efficiency.\n\nLooking at discards:\n\nSafe discards: avoid feeding opponents' potential waits.\n\nFrom previous player\u2019s melds: [W2,W3,W4] and [B2,B3,B4] \u2014 so they completed two chows in low numbers, suggesting they may be going for lower suit or mixed straight.\n\nThey discarded: T9, B3, T6, T4, B2, Chi W3, J3, W5, B4, Chi B3, W3, J2\n\nSo they completed chows in W2-W3-W4 and B2-B3-B4 \u2014 very likely collecting low sequences.\n\nAlso, they have no more melds shown, so possibly building hand with more chows.\n\nDangerous to discard W1, W5, B1, B5? But W5 is gone, B5 I hold.\n\nI have B5 \u2014 should I discard it?\n\nUnseen B5: 2 remain unseen, 2 used \u2014 4 total. Previous player has one B3, one B4, and completed [B2,B3,B4] \u2014 used one B3, one B4. They also played B2 and B4 \u2014 so likely don\u2019t have B5 now.\n\nBut another player might.\n\nSimilarly, my seat wind is West (F3). I don\u2019t have a F3 pung, but I have J3 \u2014 dragon.\n\nDragon Pung: 2 points if I can make J3 into a pung.\n\nCurrently J3: I have one, unseen: 3 \u2014 so three others out there.\n\nCould aim to collect J3, but risky.\n\nBetter to focus on finishing hand.\n\nBack to hand: I have two W8s \u2014 a pair. Could become eye.\n\nThen need two more melds.\n\nCurrent melds: [W4,W5,W6] and [W5,W5,W5] \u2014 wait, **this is invalid**.\n\nBecause the chow [W4,W5,W6] requires one W5.\n\nThe pung [W5,W5,W5] requires three W5s.\n\nThat\u2019s four W5s \u2014 okay.\n\nBut in the chow, the W5 is part of a sequence \u2014 fine.\n\nBut now, do I have any other Characters?\n\nIn hand: W8(2), W9(1) \u2014 so potentially a chow of W7,W8,W9 if I get W7.\n\nW7: unseen: 4 \u2014 all available.\n\nSo possible.\n\nIf I can form W7-W8-W9 chow, then I have:\n\n- Meld 1: [W4,W5,W6]\n- Meld 2: [W5,W5,W5] \u2014 but again, tile conflict.\n\nNo: the pung [W5,W5,W5] uses three W5s.\n\nThe chow [W4,W5,W6] uses one W5.\n\nTotal: four W5s \u2014 all used.\n\nBut then I cannot use W5 in two places \u2014 but since they are separate tiles, it's allowed.\n\nHowever, having both a pung and a chow in the same number is extremely rare and inefficient.\n\nMoreover, in terms of patterns:\n\n- Is there a pattern that rewards multiple W5s? Not really.\n\n- But **All Fives** (16 points): requires all melds and pair to contain W5/B5/T5.\n\nI have:\n- [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 contains W5 \u2192 qualifies\n- [W5,W5,W5] \u2014 qualifies\n- If I make [T5,T5,T5] or similar \u2014 but I don\u2019t have T5\n- Hand has B5, T9, etc. \u2014 no T5\n\nAnd I would need all four melds and pair to include a five.\n\nCurrently, two melds qualify.\n\nBut I have B5 in hand \u2014 could make a chow like B4,B5,B6 \u2014 but I don\u2019t have B4/B6.\n\nAnd pair would need to be a five \u2014 e.g., B5 pair.\n\nBut I only have one B5.\n\nSo hard to complete.\n\nMoreover, \u201cAll Fives\u201d requires **every** meld and the pair to contain a five \u2014 strict.\n\nSo unless I can convert everything, unlikely.\n\nGiven inefficiency of having both [W4,W5,W6] and [W5,W5,W5], perhaps the latter is a mistake.\n\nBut according to game history: I performed \"Chi W5\" (to form [W4,W5,W6]), then later \"Peng W5\" \u2014 meaning I must have acquired three W5s to form a pung.\n\nBut after forming the chow, I would have used one W5. To Peng, I need three W5s in hand \u2014 but I only had one W5 in hand before Peng? Unlikely.\n\nMore plausible: the \"Peng W5\" refers to forming a pung of W5, which required me to have two W5s in hand and claim a discard.\n\nBut I already used one W5 in the chow \u2014 so total W5s in my possession: two in hand + one in chow? No \u2014 melds are public.\n\nOnce a chow is formed, those tiles are exposed.\n\nSo when I form [W4,W5,W6] via Chi, I place those three tiles face-up.\n\nThen later, if I want to Peng W5, I need to have two W5s in hand \u2014 but I already used one W5 in the chow \u2014 so unless I had three W5s initially, I can't have two left.\n\nContradiction.\n\nTherefore, the only logical conclusion is that the meld [W5,W5,W5] is **not** a separate pung, or the chow does not contain W5.\n\nBut \"Chi W5\" means completing the chow [W4,W5,W6], which includes W5.\n\nUnless the notation is wrong.\n\nPerhaps \"Chi W5\" means I claimed a W5 to complete a chow, such as [W5,W6,W7] or [W3,W4,W5]? But the meld is recorded as [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 so yes, it contains W5.\n\nThus, the system allows it \u2014 we proceed under the assumption that it is valid (perhaps a bug, but accepted).\n\nSo I have both melds.\n\nNow, back to decision.\n\nHand: W8(2), W9(1), T9(1), B2(1), B5(1), B9(1), J3(1)\n\nOptions:\n\n- Discard B2: B2 is low bamboo. Previous player has [B2,B3,B4], so likely doesn't need B2. Safe.\n- Discard B5: mid-dots. No one showing dots chows. Possibly safe.\n- Discard W9: high character. Could help someone with W7-W8 or W8-W9 chow.\n- Discard W8: I have two \u2014 discarding one leaves a singleton. Risky if someone needs W8.\n- Discard B9: high dot. Terminal.\n- Discard T9: high bamboo. Also terminal.\n- Discard J3: White Dragon. Honor. Someone might be collecting dragons.\n\nConsider safety based on discards:\n\n- Previous player discarded J3 and J2 \u2014 so likely not collecting dragons.\n- Across player discarded J2 \u2014 so maybe not.\n- Next player hasn't discarded dragons.\n- But dragons are dangerous to keep late.\n\nJ3: I have one, unseen: 3 \u2014 so popular.\n\nBut if no one is collecting, safer.\n\nW8: I have two. If I keep both, could be eye.\n\nW9: only one \u2014 less valuable.\n\nSimilarly, T9: only one.\n\nB9: only one.\n\nB2: only one.\n\nB5: only one.\n\nSo best to keep W8 pair as potential eye.\n\nThen aim to form melds with other tiles.\n\nBut I already have two melds \u2014 need two more.\n\nPossible chow: W7-W8-W9 \u2014 if I keep W8/W9 and draw W7.\n\nOr W8-W9 and need W7.\n\nW7 is common \u2014 4 unseen.\n\nSo promising.\n\nAlternatively, discard a non-Character tile to focus on Character flush.\n\nTiles not in Character: T9, B2, B5, B9, J3\n\nKeep W8 pair, W9.\n\nDiscard one of the others.\n\nAmong them, which is safest?\n\n- B2: previous player has [B2,B3,B4] \u2014 they may have completed it, so B2 is safe.\n- B5: no one showing interest \u2014 safe.\n- B9: terminal \u2014 could be part of 789 chow.\n- T9: same.\n- J3: honor \u2014 always slightly dangerous.\n\nAlso, consider patterns.\n\nIf I go for **Full Flush** in Characters, I need all tiles in Characters.\n\nCurrently have:\n- Melds: both in W \u2014 good.\n- Hand: W8, W8, W9 \u2014 good.\n- But also T9, B2, B5, B9, J3 \u2014 bad.\n\nSo I should discard all non-Characters.\n\nFirst discard: choose the least useful.\n\nT9, B2, B5, B9, J3 \u2014 all equally bad for flush.\n\nBut B2 is a lower tile, less likely to be needed for high chows.\n\nAnd previous player already used B2 in chow \u2014 so probably safe.\n\nSimilarly, B5 is mid \u2014 could be part of 456 or 567.\n\nSlightly riskier.\n\nT9: could complete 789 for someone.\n\nB9: same.\n\nJ3: dragon \u2014 general danger.\n\nSo safest: B2 or B5.\n\nBut B2 was used by previous player \u2014 even better.\n\nSo discard B2.\n\nBut let's see valid actions: \"Play B2\" is an option.\n\nAlternatively, if I think I can go for **Outside Hand**, which values terminals and honors.\n\nMy hand has:\n- W8: not terminal (8)\n- W9: terminal\n- T9: terminal\n- B9: terminal\n- J3: honor\n- B2: not terminal (2)\n- B5: not terminal\n\nSo only W9, T9, B9, J3 are terminals/honors.\n\nW8 is not terminal \u2014 weak for Outside Hand.\n\nBetter to go for flush by discarding non-W tiles.\n\nThus, priority: discard non-Character tiles.\n\nFirst, pick one: B2, B5, B9, T9, J3\n\nWhich to keep?\n\nNone are helpful for Character hand.\n\nBut if I keep multiple, I can't win.\n\nSo must discard.\n\nReason: Minimize opponent advantage and work towards a coherent hand. Since I have two melds in Characters and a strong start in high Characters (W8 pair, W9), pursuing a **Full Flush** (24 points) is viable if I shed non-suit tiles. Among the discards, **B2** is safest because the previous player recently used B2 in a chow ([B2,B3,B4]), indicating they likely don't need another. Other options like T9, B9, or J3 are more dangerous as terminals/honors.\n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
