 Flush** (W + honors) potential. With four melds and a pair needed, I likely need to form a pair soon.\n\nLooking at possible waits:\n- If I keep W8 and discard others, could aim for **W8 pair**, completing hand around W8 pair.\n- Alternatively, I might be waiting on a **W6**, **W7**, or **W9** for edge/closed waits within the sequence\u2014but those are risky since many W-tiles are already out.\n\nBut note: I already have **[W4,W5,W6]**, **[W5,W5,W5]**, **[W7,W8,W9]** \u2014 this overlaps W5 heavily. Wait types?\n- Possible single wait on **W8** (to complete pair), assuming other melds stay.\n- Or, if W8 is discarded, perhaps aiming for terminal/honor pair?\n\nAlso consider scoring:\n- **Half Flush** (6 points): Likely achievable if I form a pair in W or honor.\n- **All Chows** (2 points): Already have three chows; if final meld is also a chow, and pair is suited, qualifies.\n- **One Voided Suit** (1 point): Yes \u2014 only W and some honors/side suits in hand.\n- **No Honors**? No \u2014 I have J3, and possibly forming honor pairs.\n- **Dragon Pung**? Not yet.\n- **Seat Wind (West/F3)**? No pung/kong of F3.\n- **Prevalent Wind (South/F2)**? Not held.\n\nI do not have any pungs except W5, which is exposed. Concealedness lost via Peng.\n\nBest chance: Complete **Half Flush** + **All Chows** + **One Voided Suit** = 6+2+1=9 points minimum \u2014 sufficient to win.\n\nTo reach that, I need:\n- Four chows\n- One pair of either W or honor\n- No non-W suited tiles in new melds\n\nCurrently, I have three chows. So, ideally, next meld should be a chow. But I\u2019m in discard phase \u2014 no draw yet. So focus is on shaping hand toward legal win.\n\nNow, among discards:\n- **T1, T9, B9, J3** \u2014 all non-W suited or honor. Keeping more than one hurts \"All Chows\" and \"Half Flush\" because they force reliance on mixed suits.\n- Best to minimize non-W tiles.\n\nThus, ideal pair would be **W8** (already have one), or possibly **J3** (but J2 is gone, J3 available).\n\nHowever, J3 is an honor, and if I can get a pair of J3, that works.\n\nBut let\u2019s compare safety and utility:\n\n- **Play T1**: Safe? T1 has been played by others (next player, across, prev). At least 2 discarded? Unshown: T1:2 \u2014 started with 4, so 2 seen/discarded \u2192 2 left. Not fully safe. But not dangerous.\n- **Play T9**: Similarly, T9 played by me and others. Unshown:1 \u2192 likely only 1 left. Risky to hold if someone needs it for win.\n- **Play B9**: Played by me and prev. Unshown:3 \u2192 relatively safe.\n- **Play W8**: I have one; unshown:2. Not too risky, but W is my main suit \u2014 breaking symmetry.\n- **Play J3**: Unshown:3 \u2192 very safe. Also, I don\u2019t have a pair yet.\n\nBut here's a key insight: **I cannot form a fourth chow unless I draw a tile that completes a chow involving T1, T9, B9, or J3** \u2014 all unlikely.\n\nInstead, most practical path is to **form a pair** from current singles.\n\nSo which tile to discard?\n\nBest candidate: **discard a terminal or honor that cannot form a pair easily**.\n\nLook at frequency in hand:\n- T1: 1 \u2192 no pair\n- T9: 1\n- B9: 1\n- J3: 1\n- W8: 1 \u2192 BUT I already used W8 in a chow, and there are 2 left unshown \u2014 higher chance of drawing another W8 than, say, T1.\n\nWait \u2014 can I draw a W8 later? Possibly. But I must discard now.\n\nMoreover, **W8** appears in existing chow [W7,W8,W9], so having a pair of W8 would make sense structurally.\n\nWhereas T1, T9, B9 are isolated.\n\nAlso, **J3**: White Dragon. Others have played J2 multiple times (all 4 gone), but J3 still has 3 unseen \u2014 safe. But again, low synergy.\n\nNow, danger of keeping non-W tiles:\n- If I keep **T1**, I may be forced to later discard it, but it blocks clean Half Flush.\n\nSame for T9, B9.\n\nSo best move: **discard one of the non-character suited tiles**.\n\nAmong T1, T9, B9 \u2014 which is least valuable?\n\nNote: Previous player has [B2,B3,B4] \u2014 a lower-end bamboo chow. They discarded B9 earlier. Unlikely going for upper bamboo. So B9 probably safe.\n\nSimilarly, T1 and T9: next player discarded T1 and T9 \u2014 so maybe not holding.\n\nBut **T9** was just played by me earlier \u2014 wait, check my history: \u201cPlay T9\u201d \u2014 yes! I discarded T9 before? No \u2014 look:\n\nMy history: ... Play J2, Play B5, Chi W8, Play J1 \u2014 no T9 discard listed.\n\nWait \u2014 initial hand had T9? And no record of playing it? Then T9 is still in hand.\n\nBut did I ever discard it? Let's trace:\n\nHand now: [W8, T1, T9, B9, J3]\n\nFrom history: I played B5, T4, F4, F3, B8, F1, Chi W5, Play T5, Play J1, Peng W5, Play T6, Play W1, Play B2, Play T2, Play J2, Play B5, Chi W8, Play J1\n\nNo T9 played. So I\u2019ve held T9 since beginning? Possible.\n\nBut others have played T9: next player played T9, and across played T9 \u2014 so at least two T9s discarded.\n\nUnshown T9:1 \u2192 only one left. So high risk if I discard mine \u2014 could feed a winning hand.\n\nSame for T1: next player played T1, I didn\u2019t \u2014 unshown:2 \u2192 safer.\n\nB9: I played B9? No \u2014 in my history: Play B3? No \u2014 first B played was B5. Then B2, then B5 again. No B9 played by me. But previous player played B9. Across played B9? No. Unshown:3 \u2014 so only one B9 played \u2014 safe to discard.\n\nJ3: I played J3? In history: Play J3 \u2014 yes! Earlier: \u201cPlay J3\u201d. So I previously discarded a J3? But now I have J3 in hand?\n\nContradiction?\n\nWait \u2014 initial hand: [W8, T1, T9, B9, J3] \u2014 five tiles.\n\nBut after a Chi or Peng, you add a tile and discard one.\n\nLet\u2019s count actions.\n\nStart: 13 tiles.\n\nEach turn: draw + discard = net 0.\n\nBut when you perform **Chi** or **Peng**, you take a discarded tile from another player, add to hand, then discard \u2014 so still 13.\n\nBut I did:\n- Chi W5 \u2192 added W5, discarded something (Play T5)\n- Peng W5 \u2192 added W5, discarded (Play T6)\n- Chi W8 \u2192 added W8, discarded (Play J1)\n\nAnd prior to that, normal plays.\n\nBut in \u201cPlay J3\u201d \u2014 that was in my history. When?\n\n\u201cPlay J3\u201d appears in my history: after Play B3, Play T6, Play T4, Play B2, then **Play J3**\n\nSo I once had and discarded a J3.\n\nNow I have **another J3**? Is that possible?\n\nYes \u2014 there are 4 identical J3 tiles.\n\nSo I discarded one J3 earlier, and now hold a different J3.\n\nUnshown J3:3 \u2192 started with 4, I discarded one, so 3 left \u2014 means none else discarded \u2192 safe.\n\nSo J3 is safe to keep or discard.\n\nBut key point: I already discarded a J3 \u2014 opponents may think I\u2019m not collecting it.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nBest strategy: Aim for **Half Flush** (W + honors), **All Chows**, **One Voided Suit**.\n\nTo maximize chances:\n- Reduce non-W tiles.\n- Try to pair up W8 or J3.\n\nCurrent hand: W8 (1), T1 (1), T9 (1), B9 (1), J3 (1)\n\nIf I discard **T1**, I keep T9, B9 \u2014 still two foreign suits.\n\nSame for discarding T9.\n\nIf I discard **B9**, same issue.\n\nBut if I discard **T1**, T9, or B9 \u2014 I still have two non-W suited tiles.\n\nOnly by discarding **two** of them can I reduce to one foreign suit.\n\nBut I can only discard one now.\n\nSo whichever I discard, I\u2019ll still have two non-W suited tiles.\n\nUnless I can draw a W or J3 and pair.\n\nBut immediate goal: avoid feeding winners, and shape toward viable score.\n\nAnother idea: **J3 pair**.\n\nI already discarded one J3 \u2014 less suspicious. Opponents may not expect me to win on J3 pair.\n\nAnd J3 is relatively safe.\n\nBut **W8 pair** is better \u2014 central to my flush.\n\nMoreover, W8 is in the same suit as all my melds \u2014 improves cohesion.\n\nSo **keep W8**, discard a non-W tile.\n\nNow choose among T1, T9, B9, J3 to discard.\n\nAvoid discarding **T9**: two already discarded (by me? no \u2014 I haven\u2019t \u2014 but next and across have), unshown:1 \u2014 dangerous.\n\nSimilarly, **T1**: two discarded \u2014 unshown:2 \u2014 safer.\n\n**B9**: only one discarded \u2014 unshown:3 \u2014 safest.\n\n**J3**: I discarded one \u2014 others haven\u2019t \u2014 so three left \u2014 safe.\n\nBut **B9** and **T1** are terminals \u2014 if I keep them, they support **Outside Hand** (8 points) if all groups contain terminals or honors.\n\nCheck current melds:\n- [W4,W5,W6]: middle numbers \u2014 not terminal \u2192 disqualifies Outside Hand\n- [W5,W5,W5]: W5 \u2014 middle \u2192 not terminal\n- [W7,W8,W9]: contains W9 \u2014 terminal\n\nSo already have two melds without terminals \u2192 **cannot qualify for Outside Hand**\n\nTherefore, **no benefit** in keeping terminals for that pattern.\n\nHence, **T1, T9, B9** are useless for Outside Hand.\n\nSo better to discard them.\n\nNow, **J3** is honor \u2014 could help:\n- Dragon Pung (if I get three)\n- Two Dragon Pungs (with another)\n- Half Flush (honors allowed)\n- Mixed Double Chow, etc.\n\nBut currently only one.\n\nStill, **J3** has more potential than T1/T9/B9.\n\nAlso, **Self Drawn**, **Single Wait**, etc., depend on how I win.\n\nBest choice: **discard B9**\n\nWhy B9?\n- Least connected to anything\n- Not helping any potential chow\n- Bamboo suit \u2014 previous player has [B2,B3,B4], so lower end; B9 is far away\n- Unseen:3 \u2014 very safe to discard\n- Removing a non-W, non-honor tile\n\nAfter discarding B9, hand becomes: [W8, T1, T9, J3] \u2014 still messy, but B9 is cleanest to go.\n\nAlternative: discard T1 \u2014 similar logic.\n\nBut T1 could potentially form a chow with T2,T3 \u2014 but I have no T2 or T3. Unlikely.\n\nSame for T9.\n\nBut B9 is pure deadwood.\n\nMoreover, I already have a W8 \u2014 keeping it supports possible W8 pair.\n\nFinal decision: **Play B9**\n\nAnswer format required.\n\nReason: Minimizes foreign suit clutter, maintains possibility of Half Flush + All Chows, removes a terminal not contributing to any viable pattern, and is safe to discard.\n\nAnswer: Play B9\n\n---\n\nSituation: I hold five single tiles: W8, T1, T9, B9, J3, with three melds all in Characters (W4\u2013W6 chow, W5 pung, W7\u2013W9 chow), indicating a strong Half Flush potential. Non-Character tiles (T1, T9, B9) hinder this pattern, while J3 offers minor honor flexibility. Tile visibility and game history show B9 is abundant and safe to discard.\n\nEvaluation: Discarding B9, T1, or T9 removes deadwood; B9 is least useful as it contributes to no potential chow or pair. Keeping W8 supports a future pair in the main suit. J3 has slight value as a dragon tile. T9 is riskier due to limited remaining copies.\n\nReason: B9 is a terminal tile in a foreign suit with no synergies, and its discard promotes a cleaner Half Flush (6 pts) + All Chows (2 pts) + One Voided Suit (1 pt) path, totaling 9 points\u2014above the 8-point threshold. It is also the safest discard.\n\nAnswer: Play B9"}
